    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-13933" textLabel="nicotianamine synthase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="A2XFU4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="1600.0" centerY="1031.1671142578125" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi, O-27, pujar">|FRAME: EC-2.5.1.43 EC 2.5.1.43, nicotianamine synthase| (NAS), is a key enzyme in the biosynthetic pathway of the mugineic acid family phytosiderophores, which are crucial for iron acquisition in graminaceous plants. The enzyme catalyzes the trimerization of |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-adenosyl-L-methionine| to form one molecule of |FRAME: CPD-463 L-nicotianamine|. Three cDNA clones encoding the enzyme, |FRAME: G-10635 NAS1|, |FRAME: G-10639 NAS2|, and |FRAME: G-10641 NAS3|, were isolated rom Fe-deficient rice roots using the barley |FRAME: G-10642 NAS1| gene as a probe |CITS: [11169192]|. |FRAME: G-10635 NAS1| and |FRAME: G-10639 NAS2| are highly homologous and are also similar to the barley gene, while |FRAME: G-10641 NAS3| is more divergent. To confrm the enzymatic activity of the |FRAME: G-10635 NAS1| gene product, it was expressed as a maltose-binding protein fusion in |FRAME: TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;|, and the recombinant protein was purified. The purified protein was assayed for NAS activity by TLC, and found to have a similar activity to the barley enzyme |CITS: [11169192]|. The activity of NAS2 and NAS3 was not assayed. Noerthern blot analysis has shown that in contrast to barley, in which Fe deficiency induces the expression of NAS genes only in roots, Fe deficiency in rice induced NAS gene expression in both roots and chlorotic leaves |CITS: [11169192]|. When grown with sufficient iron, |FRAME: G-10635 NAS1| and |FRAME: G-10639 NAS2| transcripts were detected only in roots, but their levels increased markedly in both roots and leaves in response to Fe deficiency. In contrast, the |FRAME: G-10641 NAS3| transcript was present in leaves but was very low in roots of Fe-sufficient plants. In response to Fe deficiency its expression was induced in roots but was suppressed in leaves |CITS: [14617093]|. |FRAME: G-10635 NAS1| and |FRAME: G-10639 NAS2| are expressed in Fe-sufficient roots in companion cells and pericycle cells adjacent to the protoxylem. Upon Fe deficiency, expression extended to all root cells. |FRAME: G-10641 NAS3| expression is restricted to the pericycle and companion cells of the roots, and in companion cells of leaves, irrespective of Fe status. These finding suggest that in rice the enzymes are involved in long-distance transport of Fe in addition to their roles in phytosiderophore secretion from roots |CITS: [14617093]|.</Comment>
